From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --reg-locals keeps the SSE and MMX registers in
 locals too

The generated code names the SIMD registers xmm0..xmm7 and mm0..mm7,
macros for the globals g_xmm0.. and g_mm0... Every guest access is a
volatile access the compiler must assume aliases them, so each SSE or
MMX operation stored its result to the global and the next one loaded
it again. On a PowerPC host, where the register unions keep the x86's
byte order, that was a byte-reversed store and load per lane: a 3x4
matrix product (16 movss/movhps, 12 mulps, 8 addps) compiled to 1217
instructions.

--reg-locals now treats them like the integer registers: a local
RecompXmm/RecompMmx per register a function names, loaded where memory
was the truth and written back, when written, before a call, a tail
call or a return. The XMM_*/MMX_* helpers take their operands by value,
so a register passed to one counts as read (an integer register used in
an address inside one, XMM_MEM(esi), no longer counts as written
either); XMM_LOAD_LOW/HIGH assign their first argument, and a write to a
lane (xmm0.f[0] = ...) is a write to the register. A register whose
address is taken runs against memory, as before.

A SIMD register is kept in a local only when the body first assigns it
whole (xmm0 = ..., the register not named on the right). One the body
first reads, or writes in part (movss to a register, movhps), would be
loaded where the body starts and its lanes held through every guest
access to the write-back: in a mostly scalar function that uses one
such register for a few operations, that spilled more than it saved
(Halo's sub_00086F50: +156 instructions). It stays in its global. And
a SIMD register written in a straight run of code goes back to its
global where the run ends (before a label or a branch), instead of at
the next call or return: a few SSE operations inside a scalar loop
otherwise held every lane they wrote live around the whole loop.

The same expressions
compute the same values; only the loads and stores go away.
